From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] vrend: new textures zeroed

Windows' drivers give new video memory zeroed, and apps come to depend on it: GravityMark's Direct3D 11 renderer
samples a shadow map (a 4096x4096 2-layer depth texture) it never renders or clears, which is zero depth - lit - on
Windows. The host driver leaves a new texture with whatever its memory held: NaN depths in one layer, every
comparison failing, the sun's light gone and the exposure hunting (the scene flat, too bright, flickering). New
textures are now cleared to zero (glClearTexImage, each level). Sparse textures have no memory of their own, and
compressed formats can't be cleared (they come with their data). This also keeps one guest's texture memory from
showing another's leftovers.
---
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c	2026-10-26 13:49:22.376962488 +0100
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_renderer.c	2026-10-26 16:47:13.910229095 +0100
@@ -9138,6 +9138,16 @@
       glTexParameteri(gr->target, GL_TEXTURE_MAX_LEVEL, pr->last_level);
    }
 
+   /* Zeroed, as Windows' drivers give new video memory: apps may sample a texture they never drew or uploaded
+    * (GravityMark's Direct3D 11 renderer: a shadow map it doesn't render, where zero depths mean lit), and the host
+    * driver leaves a new texture with whatever its memory held (NaN depths there: everything in shadow). Sparse
+    * textures have no memory of their own; compressed formats can't be cleared (and come with their data) */
+   if (!image_oes && !vrend_state.use_gles && has_feature(feat_clear_texture) && glformat &&
+       !(gr->tiled & VIRGL_BIND_SPARSE) && !util_format_is_compressed(pr->format)) {
+      for (level = 0; level <= pr->last_level; level++)
+         glClearTexImage(gr->gl_id, level, glformat, gltype, NULL);
+   }
+
    /* where a sparse texture's mip tail begins (the levels from it on bind together) */
    if (gr->tiled & VIRGL_BIND_SPARSE)
       glGetTexParameteriv(gr->target, GL_NUM_SPARSE_LEVELS_ARB, &gr->sparse_levels);
